<<PlayBackgroundMusic emotional_1>>
<<SetBGSprite "HutInterior">>

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Smiling">>
CoachDev: Namaste, ShivPrasad. I'm Dev.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Proud">>
Shiv Prasad: Namaste. Sit. Tea?

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Not for me, thank you—I know you leave for the mill soon, so I'll be brief and honest.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Proud">>
Shiv Prasad: Brief and honest pair well—say.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: I watched your younger boy today—Raju sees the ball early and carries himself steady, which is rarer than runs. I run an academy in Matunga and want him to come for structured training.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Proud">>
Shiv Prasad: Matunga sounds far when pockets are near-empty.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: I understand the worry—there are fee waivers for boys who earn them, and I can get him a trial first with no promises beyond that.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Trial is also cost—bus fare is not air, time is a wage, and who will walk him when his mother washes floors and I watch gates?

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: True, but sometimes the city makes space for a boy who won't waste it—we can arrange morning batches or late evening, and I can escort him the first few times.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Sir, notes do not bend night shifts, and your feet won't always be free—tomorrow when the bus breaks or coin is short, who carries him then?

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Many boys come with less and make it through because a father said yes once, even when the maths looked ugly.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Many boys also come back with less—I have seen scouts who took money for "trials" and promises that ate our rice.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: I won't ask for a rupee for a trial—if anyone does, you call me first, and we will manage the rest.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: And if he enters your ground and the world enters his head—big places make small boys feel like kings, then the fall is long.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Raju doesn't strut—he thanks umpires, shares water, and your elder one taught him that foundation. I'm offering a door to learning, not promising stardom.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Doors creak, hinges cost oil—the world speaks sweet when it wants something, so what do you want?

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: I want to see whether a boy with a clear mind and quick hands can grow where there is better light—and if he can, we do not let poverty steal his chance.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Poverty steals quietly because it knows our house—fourteen months ago I agreed to routine here at dawn, and that was my "yes" without bus fare or new fear.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: And it gave him muscles memory can trust, but the next inch needs better bowling and correction—same bell, same breath, just longer shadows.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Longer shadows also hide things—if Matunga takes him far and he returns late, his studies slip and the city counts our slips loudly.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Allow me one month with two sessions a week—I'll pick him up from the banyan, and if your heart says no after that, I step back.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: My heart already says no, sir—it says feed the house, not the dream, and keep the boy near until the world proves it won't swallow him.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: I respect a father who protects his own—may I leave a number in case the sums change, and I'll just answer without pushing.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Leave the number—numbers don't eat, words sometimes do.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Here. No pressure. Only possibility.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Serious">>
Shiv Prasad: Sir, I thank you for the respect and for speaking to me as if my "no" matters—for now it's no, completely.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Understood.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Proud">>
Shiv Prasad: May your boys play well and mine sleep well—both are victories where we live.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: True—I'll take your leave, and Ramu, you're raising him well.

<<SetCharacterExpression "Ramu" "Neutral">>
Ramu: Yes, sir.

<<SetCharacterExpression "CoachDev" "Serious">>
CoachDev: Good night, ShivPrasad-ji.

<<SetCharacterExpression "ShivPrasad" "Proud">>
Shiv Prasad: Good night.

<<HideAllCharacters>>
<<EndEvent>>
<<StopBackgroundMusic>>
